<BlogArticle
	title="How to Resize a Photo for a PAN Card: Size, Pixels and KB"
	description="Learn how to resize a PAN card photo, understand dimensions, pixels, DPI and KB limits, and verify the current Form 93/94 upload rules."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/resize-pan-card-photo/"
	heading="How to resize a photo for a PAN card application"
	intro="A suitable PAN photo must match the application’s current image rules and remain clear after resizing. Dimensions, pixel count, resolution and file size are related, but they are not interchangeable."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Check the current application first</h2>
		<p>
			Before starting an NSDL photo resize or choosing a UTIITSL preset,
			identify the form you are actually submitting. As of 1 April 2026,
			Protean says Indian applicants should use the revised Form 93 or
			Form 94 for a new PAN application; UTIITSL also lists those new
			forms and says older Form 49A applications are no longer accepted.
			The current upload flow, not an old tutorial or a preset label,
			decides which dimensions and file limit to follow.
		</p>
		<p>
			Older guides often quote a Protean/NSDL photo of 2.5 × 3.5 cm at
			200 DPI and up to 50 KB, or a UTIITSL photo of 213 × 213 pixels at
			300 DPI and under 30 KB. Treat these as legacy reference presets
			unless the current form displays the same requirements.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">PAN card photo size in KB, pixels and DPI</h2>
		<p>
			Width and height in pixels describe how many picture elements make
			up the image. DPI describes how those pixels map to a printed
			dimension; changing DPI metadata alone does not add detail or
			change the pixel count. Kilobytes (KB) measure the saved file’s
			size, which is affected by pixel dimensions, image detail, color,
			and JPEG compression. A photo can have the correct pixel size and
			still exceed the allowed KB.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">A practical resizing workflow</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Open the official application and note its current photo format, aspect ratio, pixel/DPI requirements, and maximum file size.</li>
			<li>Choose a recent, sharp, well-lit color photo with a plain background. Avoid a screenshot or a photo of a printed photo if a clean original is available.</li>
			<li>Use a <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card photo resizer</a> to select the photo, set the requested aspect ratio, and crop around the head and shoulders as instructed.</li>
			<li>Set the exact pixel dimensions if the application specifies them. Export as the required format, usually JPEG only when the form asks for JPEG.</li>
			<li>Check the downloaded file’s dimensions, file type and actual size on your device. Reopen it to ensure the face remains sharp and the background looks clean.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Avoid common PAN photo mistakes</h2>
		<p>
			Do not assume that “passport size,” a particular DPI, or a 50 KB
			limit applies to every route. Do not stretch a small image to
			create artificial detail, and do not keep lowering JPEG quality
			until the face looks blocky. If the correct crop cannot meet the
			limit without becoming unclear, start with a better source image.
		</p>
		<p>
			This independent PAN card document resizer online prepares an
